Ezero 0.1 - an easy language built on Zero

Zero remains the existing system language. Ezero adds automatically managed
values, text without required quotes, typed phrases, lists, functions and
simple control actions. The frontend, package writer and runtime are original
Zero code, built by the existing self-hosted Zero compiler. No external
language engine, compiler backend or runtime library is used.

The hosted workflow below runs on Linux x86-64. The same original frontend,
packer and runtime also run inside Zero OS through its native32/64-bit
memory-stream application ABI. The boot ISO includes both architectures in
TOOLS.BIN; no Linux or firmware service is used by those guest applications.
Guest execution shares kernel memory and is not process isolation. See
../portability/guest/ABI.txt and ../portability/guest/BUNDLE.txt.

Build and run
From the zero-foundation project directory, with build/zero already present:

    bash ezero/build.sh
    bash ezero/run.sh ezero/examples/hello.ezero

Expected output:
    Hello
    Hello
    Hello

To keep an executable and its generated Zero source:

    bash ezero/compile.sh ezero/examples/banana.ezero build/banana build/banana.zero
    ./build/banana

compile.sh accepts INPUT.ezero OUTPUT and an optional EMITTED.zero path.
It publishes outputs only after compilation succeeds. Source and output paths
must differ. run.sh uses a temporary executable and removes it afterwards.
If the original Zero compiler has not been built, first run bash build.sh.

A first program
    Mira => name
    Hello name => show

Mira is unquoted text. name becomes a binding, and its later use reads the
stored value. Hello name forms a phrase and prints Hello Mira.

Numbers remain numbers inside phrases
    3 банана => purchase
    purchase => show
    purchase[1] + 2 => show
    purchase[2] => show
    size(purchase[2]) => show

Output is 3 банана, then 5, then банана, then 6, each on its own line.
The first element is the number 3; the second is UTF-8 text. Collection indices
start at 1. Index 0 and indices beyond size are errors.

Ezero and Zero, the same observable result
Ezero:
    3 => repeat [
        Hello => show
    ]

Zero:
    0 => @count.
    0 => @position.
    "Hello\n" => @message.
    ?? @count < 3 [
        0 -> @position.
        ?? :load8[@message + @position] != 0 [
            !put[:load8[@message + @position]].
            @position + 1 -> @position.
        ]
        @count + 1 -> @count.
    ]

Both print Hello three times. Ezero owns the traversal, output formatting and
value storage. The matching source files are examples/hello.ezero and
examples/hello.zero. See LANGUAGE.txt for lists, functions, controls and the
exact text/name rules. Other runnable examples are in examples/README.txt.

How the current implementation works
The Ezero frontend validates source and produces a bounded instruction/data
package. pack.zero emits its initialization as ordinary Zero source. The
build wrapper combines that initialization with the original Zero runtime;
the existing Zero compiler produces one standalone Linux executable.
This first version embeds our instruction interpreter in that executable.
It does not directly translate every Ezero expression into native instructions.
There is no separate installed language runtime needed to execute the result.

Values have automatic runtime types. Immutable text, lists and phrases live
in a copying garbage collector with two 262144-byte (256 KiB) semispaces. The
live objects plus a requested allocation must fit one space. Exhaustion is a
reported error; memory is managed automatically but is not unlimited.
MEMORY.txt gives the frame, stack, heap and collection contracts.

Verification and current scope
The integrated suite passed 85 checks through the real frontend, generated
Zero program and runtime. The frontend additionally passed 522 structural,
capacity and malformed-input checks. These are bounded tests, not proof of
absence of all defects. Reproduce the integrated suite with:

    bash ezero/tests/run.sh

Host shell and Python arrange builds and check results; they are not Ezero
parsing or execution implementations. No source or components were taken from
the internet for this language implementation.

Current Ezero has no modules, closures, classes, guest file/window/network APIs,
exceptions, threads, floating-point numbers, signed numbers or division.
Native guest compilation and execution are verified separately in
../verification/guest-app/bundle-merged/. Persistent source/project files and
protected application execution remain further work.
Existing Zero and Zero OS sources remain the foundation; Ezero does not
replace or rename their language or compiler.

Host files and program arguments
The0.10 compiler/runtime supports host(),arguments(),
readfile(path),writefile(path|text),fileexists(path). See HOST.txt for exact
limits and examples/host-files.ezero for a file-copy example. writefile creates
a NEW file only and preserves every existing destination. Host operations
except host() report a source-line error in Zero OS; host() permits a guard.

Native compiler commands on Linux and Windows
The native toolkit supplies zero/ezero (zero.exe/ezero.exe on Windows):
    ezero INPUT.ezero NEW_OUTPUT
These original Zero commands perform the pipeline in-process, with no shell,
CMD or Python runtime. The earlier shell workflow above remains source-build
orchestration. See ../tools/compiler-cli.txt and ../DISTRIBUTIONS.txt.

Text convenience
lines(text) returns a one-based list of lines, recognizing LF and CRLF.
join(list | separator) combines a list of text without manual loops. Both
work on Linux, Windows and Zero OS; files can be composed with readfile and
writefile on hosts. See LANGUAGE.txt for empty lines, strict types and limits,
and examples/text.ezero for a runnable example.
The external behavioral regression suite is ezero/tests/text.py --baseline.
